  "summary": {
    "as": "Introduced in Senate",
    "asOf": "2025-03-26",
    "date": "2026-10-21T13:56:10Z",
    "text": "Personal Health Investment Today Act of 2025 or the PHIT Act of 2025\n\nThis bill allows a federal tax deduction of up to $1,000 ($2,000 for married individuals who file a joint federal tax return\u00a0or\u00a0a\u00a0head of a household) for qualified sports and fitness expenses. (Conditions apply.)\n\nUnder current law, an individual may deduct unreimbursed expenses paid for medical care for themselves, their spouse, or a qualified dependent to the extent that such expenses exceed 7.5% of adjusted gross income.\n\nUnder the bill, the definition of medical care (for purposes of the tax deduction) is expanded to include qualified sports and fitness expenses. The bill defines\u00a0qualified sports and fitness expenses as amounts paid exclusively for the sole purpose of\u00a0participating in a physical activity, including\n\nfitness facility memberships,participation or instruction in physical exercise or activity, orequipment for a physical exercise or activity program."
  },
